The submission succeeded (exit_code: 0) but the target didn't crash. The trace was valid though. Let me analyze why it didn't crash and improve the PoC.

The issue is that `dict.dictLookup("Length", ...)` returns an Object. If Length is an indirect reference (99 0 R), it calls `Object::fetch` which calls `XRef::fetch(99, 0)`. In `XRef::fetch`, `getEntry(99)` is called. Since the xref table only has 6 entries (0-5), `size` is 6, and `99 >= size`, so `getEntry` calls `readXRefUntil(99)`.

In `readXRefUntil`, the while loop condition checks `untilEntryNum < size` which is `99 < 6` = false, so the loop doesn't execute. Then the post-loop check accesses `entries[untilEntryNum]` which is out of bounds!

Wait, but the submission returned exit_code 0 (no crash). Let me look at `getEntry` again:

```cpp
XRefEntry *XRef::getEntry(int i, bool complainIfMissing)
{
  if (i >= size || entries[i].type == xrefEntryNone) {
    ...
    readXRefUntil(i);
    if (unlikely(i >= size)) {
      static XRefEntry dummy;
      dummy.offset = 0;
      dummy.gen = -1;
      dummy.type = xrefEntryNone;
      dummy.flags = 0;
      return &dummy;
    }
    ...
  }
  return &entries[i];
}
```

After `readXRefUntil(99)`, it checks `i >= size` (99 >= 6) and returns a dummy entry. So `fetch` gets a dummy entry with type `xrefEntryNone`, and goes to the `default` case in the switch, which goes to `err`, which returns `Object(objNull)`.

So the Length lookup returns objNull, and `makeStream` sets `length = 0` (line 230). Then it continues normally and at line 294, `entry->setFlag(XRefEntry::Parsing, false)` is called on the valid entry pointer (since entry was obtained at line 203 before any of this happened).

The problem is that `constructXRef` is never called because `readXRefUntil` returns early due to the `untilEntryNum >= size` check.

I need a different approach. Let me think about what actually triggers the reallocation of the entries array.

